{
  "abstract": "Retaining an observation and predicting actions toward it does not establish that a learned planner can use the evidence to explore. We study this interface in the supplied Thinker benchmark while preserving learned imaginary actions, resets and planning-memory queries. A goal-conditioned extension trains a separate hindsight action prior. A further intervention retains actual actions and directly teaches the acting planner through fresh imagination, using a standard paired score-function estimator for expected conditional log likelihood. Two matched comparisons and a subsequent ordered-reference trial each use one training seed per arm, 160 million real transitions and a common 200-million-transition curiosity schedule. Explicit goal information in imagination increases fingerprint-hit counts without extending the room union. The complete extra actor update increases later-room reach from 1/64 to 42/64 games while reducing mean reward from 2153.125 to 1843.75. Those visits contain only 597 actions, with median two actions per interval; 70 of 73 entries occur during unassigned continuation. Presenting ordered observations from complete self-generated prefixes subsequently yields mean reward 207.8125, 26/64 first-room exits and no later-room reach. All five arms have zero independently replayed level completions. Shared optimizer effects, changed experience, bundled trajectory changes and one training seed limit attribution; games within a batch share novelty memory. We release exact source reconstruction, complete per-game outcomes and replayable actor/control/trajectory actions. The contribution separates available action information, internal attainment, changed occupancy and sustained control. It establishes a concrete acting-policy interface and bounded behavioral findings, not reliable return, robust exploration improvement or a task solution.",
